Abstract = {It is demonstrated that a compact monolayer of
   1,2-dioleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphocholine adsorbed to a hanging mercury
   drop electrode can serve as a simple electrochemical model system to
   study biomembrane penetration by gold nanoparticles. The hydrogen
   redox-chemistry characteristic of ligand-stabilized gold nanoparticles
   in molecularly close contact with a mercury electrode is used as an
   indicator of membrane penetration. Results for water-dispersible gold
   nanoparticles of two different sizes are reported, and comparisons are
   made with the cellular uptake of the same preparations of nanoparticles
   by a common human fibroblast cell line. The experimental system
   described here can be used to study physicochemical aspects of membrane
   penetration in the absence of complex biological mechanisms, and it
   could also be a starting point for the development of a test bed for the
   toxicity of nanomaterials.},
