   (MPI), directed by Stephen Levinson. Key ideas about pragmatic typology
   were developed at MPI in the Human Sociality and Systems of Language Use
   project directed by N.J. Enfield (European Research Council HSSLU grant
   240853), in collaboration with Mark Dingemanse, Elizabeth Manrique,
   Giovanni Rossi, Joe Blythe, Julija Baranova, and Lila San Roque and with
   input from members of the INTERACT Project (European Research Council
   INTERACT grant 269484), directed by Stephen Levinson; from additional
   MPI colleagues including Penny Brown, Olivier Le Guen, Tanya Stivers,
   Mark Sicoli, Kobin Kendrick, Gertie Hoyman, and Kaoru Hayano; and from
   MPI visitors including Paul Drew, Jorg Zinken, Ilana Mushin, Rod
   Gardner, and Chase Raymond, among many others. Thanks go to Nick
   Williams and Kris Stenzel for discussions of the role of conversational
   data in language documentation; Cecilia Rojas and Gabriela Garcia Salido
   for an invitation to talk about pragmatic typology at UNAM; and
   researchers at centers for conversation analysis research including the
   University of Helsinki, York University, UCLA, and UCSB for helpful
   meetings and discussions. Thanks also go to the Spanish language
   conversation analysis community, including Veronica Temer and Luis
   Miguel Olguin, for recent discussions. Special thanks go to the
   indigenous, Aboriginal, Creole, Deaf, and Deaf Blind communities who
   participated in the research summarized here.},
